WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.589
Lesson five.

00:00:01.589 --> 00:00:02.828
Generations.

00:00:02.828 --> 00:00:07.651
Now let's watch the population improve, one generation at a time.

00:00:08.251 --> 00:00:10.193
Each dot is one pilot.

00:00:10.193 --> 00:00:16.279
Each column is one generation of fifty pilots, and the height of a dot is its fitness.

00:00:16.279 --> 00:00:20.463
Red pilots mostly crash, and green ones mostly land.

00:00:21.063 --> 00:00:25.144
In the first twenty generations, nearly every pilot crashes.

00:00:25.144 --> 00:00:31.506
But each generation is sampled around the best of the last one, so the whole column creeps upward.

00:00:31.506 --> 00:00:35.887
The best pilot lands half its episodes by generation twenty.

00:00:36.487 --> 00:00:38.884
Then the whole population climbs.

00:00:38.884 --> 00:00:42.391
By generation fifty, more than half the pilots land.

00:00:42.391 --> 00:00:47.360
By seventy-five, almost all of them do, and by one hundred, every single one.

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:51.803
The orange line is the best pilot, and the blue line is the average.

00:00:51.803 --> 00:00:57.189
As the population agrees on one strategy, the average catches up with the best.

00:00:57.789 --> 00:01:01.513
Fitness is just a number, so let's watch the flights.

00:01:01.513 --> 00:01:09.322
Every pilot of a generation starts from exactly the same spot, with the same push, and we draw where each one goes.

00:01:09.922 --> 00:01:12.012
Generation zero is chaos.

00:01:12.012 --> 00:01:16.827
Pilots fall straight down, or fire an engine and fly off the screen.

00:01:16.827 --> 00:01:20.754
One generation later, one pilot lands, almost by luck.

00:01:20.754 --> 00:01:23.335
By generation five, none do.

00:01:23.935 --> 00:01:26.292
At generation ten, still none.

00:01:26.292 --> 00:01:27.904
Then it takes off.

00:01:27.904 --> 00:01:35.337
Three pilots land at generation twenty, fourteen at thirty, twenty-seven at forty, and thirty-six at fifty.

00:01:35.937 --> 00:01:38.572
By seventy-five, forty-eight land.

00:01:38.572 --> 00:01:45.458
By the end, all fifty, and the trails bundle into one smooth curve down to the middle of the pad.

00:01:46.058 --> 00:01:49.299
This is the same population, seen as points.

00:01:49.299 --> 00:01:51.855
Each pilot is thirty-six numbers.

00:01:51.855 --> 00:01:58.949
A method called principal component analysis flattens them down to two, so each dot is a whole pilot.

00:01:59.549 --> 00:02:06.566
The centre of the cloud, in blue, travels a long way from where it started, toward the region where pilots land.

00:02:06.566 --> 00:02:08.913
The white line is its path.

00:02:09.513 --> 00:02:11.103
Here is a neat trick.

00:02:11.103 --> 00:02:16.640
Only the highest score matters, so if you double every weight, every decision stays the same.

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:21.738
The weights grew about a hundred times bigger, while the step size stayed around one.

00:02:21.738 --> 00:02:28.726
So each step got smaller and smaller compared with the weights, and the search turned into fine-tuning on its own.
